{
  "dom_id": "step-q49092",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[0,15]$ with a regular step size. If the right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $60$, then what must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $k$ |",
  "worked_solution": "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(5)+f(10)+f(15)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+4+k)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(7+k)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5k+35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A$ must be  $60,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k+35 & =60 \\\\ 5k & =60-35 \\\\ k & =\\frac{25}{5} \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
